{%- comment -%}
  Horizon's native blocks/email-signup.liquid, rebuilt on the Mionas form snippets. Same contract:
  one {% raw %}{% form 'customer' %}{% endraw %} with a single contact[email], which is what creates
  the customer profile and its marketing consent. Everything else in the native block is appearance
  settings -- border style, integrated button, arrow-vs-text button, custom colours, type presets --
  that the design system answers with tokens instead, so none of it carries over.

  WHY THIS IS ITS OWN BLOCK AND NOT A PRESET OF blocks/mionas-form.liquid. That block's children
  cannot read the `form` object -- theme blocks receive no variables from parent code and `form` is a
  tag-scoped local -- so its errors can only ever be a banner above the fields. An email signup has
  exactly one field, and a malformed address is the only thing Shopify ever rejects, so a banner
  would put the message somewhere other than the control it belongs to. Owning the form tag here
  lets the error go straight into snippets/mionas-text-field.liquid's `error` param, which gives the
  underline, aria-invalid, aria-describedby and the autofocus. Same reasoning that kept
  blocks/mionas-contact-form.liquid off mionas-form.

  Second reason: mionas-form hardcodes the contact form type. Making that a setting would let a
  merchant point a four-field form at the customer endpoint, where only contact[email] means
  anything and every other field is silently discarded. Do not open that door.

  NO CARD OF ITS OWN. This block renders exactly two things -- the email field and a button under
  it -- with no surface, no heading, no description and no note: a merchant who wants a title, a
  carded look, or supporting copy adds a mionas-text block and/or a Mionas: Card around this one,
  which gives them the full type scale and the theme's own card surface instead of one hard-coded
  style baked into a form block. That is the same call mionas-form made when it dropped its heading
  setting, and blocks/mionas-contact-form.liquid follows the identical rule -- read that file's
  header comment too, the two should never drift apart on this point.

  The BLOCK has no surface of its own -- the outer wrapper is transparent with 0 default padding,
  so this drops cleanly into a footer, a split panel, a coloured section, or inside a Mionas: Card a
  page composes around it.

  FIELD GAP IS A SETTING, NOT A HARDCODED VALUE. `field_gap` below is forwarded to
  snippets/mionas-form.liquid's `gap` param -- the space between the field and the button. Default
  matches Form.tsx's own `<Box gap="lg">` (--space-lg / 24px).

  CLIENT-SIDE ERRORS ARE JS-DRIVEN ON PURPOSE. `validate: true` on the mionas-form render below
  loads assets/mionas-form-validation.js, which sets `novalidate` on the form FROM JAVASCRIPT and
  writes the same field/label classes and message paragraph the server-side `error` param produces,
  so the two error states are indistinguishable. Because novalidate is set from JS rather than in
  the form tag, a visitor without JavaScript keeps the browser's native validation. Do not move it
  into Liquid.

  type="email" on its own parses far too loosely -- it accepts "marc@gmail" and "a@b", which then
  submit, get rejected by Shopify and come back as a full page load, looking for all the world like
  the button ignoring the error. snippets/mionas-text-field.liquid therefore adds a stricter default
  `pattern` to every email field; that is where the regex and its reasoning live. Shopify's own
  check remains the authority for everything the pattern cannot know, and it renders through the
  very same markup, so a value that still slips past fails visibly rather than differently.

  NO MERCHANT-SETTABLE ERROR COPY. Shopify's own translated message is more accurate than anything a
  merchant would type, and it is what mionas-contact-form already shows.

  NO CONSENT CHECKBOX. Shopify records nothing from a client-side checkbox, so a required consent box
  here would produce no auditable consent record. The honest equivalent is a line of copy in a
  neighbouring mionas-text block, linking to the privacy policy.

  THE MULTI-INSTANCE GUARD IS UNVERIFIED ON THE CUSTOMER ENDPOINT. contact[id] demonstrably
  round-trips into form.id on the CONTACT endpoint -- that is what lets mionas-form and
  mionas-contact-form scope their banners. Whether the customer endpoint does the same has not been
  confirmed, and Horizon's native blocks/email-signup.liquid guards on form.id == blank alone, which
  hints that it may not. The guard below is written as if it holds: correct if it does, and harmless
  if it does not. If it does not, two email signups on one page both show the success or error
  message when either is submitted -- identical to the native block's behaviour, so a platform
  limitation rather than a regression. Do not paper over it with JavaScript: the message is
  server-rendered into a fresh document, and hiding one copy client-side would leave a screen reader
  announcing both.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% liquid
  assign block_settings = block.settings
  assign form_id = block.id | prepend: 'MionasEmailSignup-'
  assign email_field_id = form_id | append: '-email'
  assign layout_classes = 'mionas-email-signup'
  if block_settings.layout == 'inline'
    assign layout_classes = layout_classes | append: ' mionas-email-signup--inline'
  endif
  if block_settings.button_width == 'fit'
    assign layout_classes = layout_classes | append: ' mionas-email-signup--button-fit'
  endif

  # mionas-submit-button has no fallback of its own -- a merchant who clears "Button label" would
  # otherwise ship a button with no accessible name, announced by screen readers as just "button".
  assign button_label = block_settings.button_label | default: 'Suscribirme'
%}

<div
  class="{{ layout_classes }} spacing-style"
  style="
    {%- render 'spacing-style', settings: block_settings -%}
    {%- if block_settings.width == 'custom' -%}
      --mionas-email-signup-width: {{ block_settings.custom_width }}%;
    {%- endif -%}
    {%- if block_settings.width_mobile == 'custom' -%}
      --mionas-email-signup-width-mobile: {{ block_settings.custom_width_mobile }}%;
    {%- endif -%}
  "
  {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
>
  {%- form 'customer', id: form_id -%}
    <input name="contact[id]" type="hidden" value="{{ form_id }}">

    {%- if block_settings.customer_tag != blank -%}
      <input name="contact[tags]" type="hidden" value="{{ block_settings.customer_tag | escape }}">
    {%- endif -%}

    {% liquid
      # Two signups on one page would otherwise fire each other's messages: Shopify re-renders the
      # whole page after a post and every form object sees the same posted data. How far this
      # actually holds on the customer endpoint is recorded in this file's header comment -- read it
      # before relying on the guard.
      assign form_matches = false
      if form.id == form_id or form.id == blank
        assign form_matches = true
      endif

      # Shopify only ever reports an error against email here. Routed into the field's own `error`
      # param rather than a banner, so the underline, the label colour and the message all come from
      # FormField/FormLabel/FormError as the design system composes them.
      # Kept on one line: inside a liquid tag every newline ends the statement, so a filter chain
      # wrapped across lines is a syntax error, not a continuation.
      assign email_error = ''
      if form.errors and form_matches
        assign email_error = form.errors.translated_fields.email | capitalize | append: ' ' | append: form.errors.messages.email
      endif

      # Assigned inside the form tag, not above it: the form object is scoped to the tag, so reading
      # form.email outside yields nothing and the field comes back empty after a failed submit --
      # exactly when the user least wants to retype it. Whether the customer endpoint echoes email
      # back at all is unconfirmed; if it does not, this is simply blank and nothing breaks.
      assign email_value = form.email
    %}

    {%- if form.posted_successfully? and form_matches -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        A banner ABOVE the fields, which stay in place -- matching blocks/mionas-form.liquid. It does
        not replace the form body: consistency with the other two Mionas forms wins, and the form
        staying visible keeps the block editable in the theme editor after a test submit.

        autofocus is load-bearing: after a post the page is a fresh document with focus on <body> and
        no announcement that anything happened. Moving focus here is what a screen reader reports.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="mionas-email-signup__success" tabindex="-1" autofocus>
        {{- 'icon-checkmark.svg' | inline_asset_content -}}
        {{ block_settings.success_text }}
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- capture email_signup_fields -%}
      {%- render 'mionas-text-field',
        label: block_settings.email_label,
        name: 'contact[email]',
        id: email_field_id,
        type: 'email',
        value: email_value,
        placeholder: block_settings.email_placeholder,
        autocomplete: 'email',
        spellcheck: 'false',
        autocapitalize: 'off',
        error: email_error
      -%}

      {%- render 'mionas-submit-button', label: button_label -%}
    {%- endcapture -%}

    {%- liquid
      # The render tag does not accept filters on its arguments, so the px suffix has to be applied
      # here first.
      assign field_gap_css = block_settings.field_gap | append: 'px'
    -%}
    {%- render 'mionas-form', content: email_signup_fields, validate: true, gap: field_gap_css -%}
  {%- endform -%}
</div>

{% stylesheet %}
  /* The BLOCK has no surface -- see the header comment.

     flex: 1 1 auto with width: 100% keeps the block from collapsing when a parent section stacks to
     a column on mobile; the desktop override zeroes flex-basis so a row splits by the sibling widths
     instead of by this 100%. blocks/mionas-form.liquid and blocks/mionas-contact-form.liquid both
     carry this same pattern -- check all three when tuning it. */
  .mionas-email-signup {
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    min-width: 0;
    width: var(--mionas-email-signup-width, 100%);
    font-family: var(--font-family-archivo);
  }

  @media screen and (max-width: 749px) {
    .mionas-email-signup {
      width: var(--mionas-email-signup-width-mobile, var(--mionas-email-signup-width, 100%));
    }
  }

  @media screen and (min-width: 750px) {
    .mionas-email-signup {
      flex-basis: 0;
    }
  }

  .mionas-email-signup__success {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-xs);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
  }

  /* Button width, stacked layout. align-self is not optional alongside width: fit-content --
     snippets/mionas-form.liquid's flex column stretches its children by default, so without it the
     button would still span the full width and this setting would appear to do nothing.

     The inline layout below overrides width regardless: a full-width button in a row would leave no
     space for the field, so inline has one sensible answer and takes it. That makes this setting a
     no-op there, which is intended, not a conflict. */
  .mionas-email-signup--button-fit .mionas-submit-button {
    width: fit-content;
    align-self: flex-start;
  }

  /* The inline layout is a MODIFIER, not a second markup path: the stacked column is
     snippets/mionas-form.liquid's own flex column and stays untouched. Only three things change --
     the direction, the field taking the free space, and the button's width:100% from
     snippets/mionas-submit-button.liquid.

     align-items: flex-start, NOT flex-end. The field's wrapper (snippets/mionas-form-field.liquid)
     grows DOWNWARD when it reports an error, so bottom-aligning would shove the button down with it
     every time validation fails. Top-aligning keeps the button still. The button is shorter than the
     field, so it sits at the field's top edge -- accepted, and part of why this is not the default
     layout.

     Below 750px this rule does not apply at all, so inline falls back to the stacked column. That is
     deliberate: an input and a button side by side on a phone leaves neither usable. */
  @media screen and (min-width: 750px) {
    .mionas-email-signup--inline .mionas-form {
      flex-direction: row;
      align-items: flex-start;
    }

    .mionas-email-signup--inline .mionas-form-field {
      flex: 1 1 auto;
    }

    .mionas-email-signup--inline .mionas-submit-button {
      width: auto;
      flex: 0 0 auto;
    }
  }
{% endstylesheet %}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Mionas: email signup",
  "tag": null,
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Signups create a customer profile with marketing consent, the same as Shopify's built-in email signup."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "email_label",
      "label": "Email label",
      "default": "Email"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "email_placeholder",
      "label": "Email placeholder",
      "default": "tu@email.com"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "button_label",
      "label": "Button label",
      "default": "Suscribirme"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "button_width",
      "label": "Button width",
      "options": [
        { "value": "fill", "label": "Fill" },
        { "value": "fit", "label": "Fit to text" }
      ],
      "default": "fill",
      "info": "Applies to the stacked layout. The inline layout always fits the button to its text, so the card has room."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "success_text",
      "label": "Success message",
      "default": "¡Gracias! Ya estás suscrito."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "customer_tag",
      "label": "Customer tag",
      "info": "Optional. Tags every signup in Shopify admin so they can be segmented, e.g. \"newsletter\". Leave blank to match Shopify's built-in block, which adds no tag. Separating values with commas creates multiple tags."
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "field_gap",
      "label": "Gap between field and button",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 60,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 24,
      "info": "24px matches the design system's own Form spacing (--space-lg)."
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Layout"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "layout",
      "label": "Layout",
      "options": [
        { "value": "stacked", "label": "Stacked" },
        { "value": "inline", "label": "Inline" }
      ],
      "default": "stacked",
      "info": "Inline puts the field and the button on one row on screens 750px and wider. Below that it stacks either way."
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "width",
      "label": "Width",
      "options": [
        { "value": "fill", "label": "Fill available space" },
        { "value": "custom", "label": "Custom" }
      ],
      "default": "fill",
      "info": "Fill lets the block share a row with its siblings and split the space between them. A custom width overrides that."
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "custom_width",
      "label": "Width",
      "min": 20,
      "max": 100,
      "step": 5,
      "unit": "%",
      "default": 100,
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.width == 'custom' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "width_mobile",
      "label": "Width on mobile",
      "options": [
        { "value": "fill", "label": "Fill available space" },
        { "value": "custom", "label": "Custom" }
      ],
      "default": "fill"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "custom_width_mobile",
      "label": "Width on mobile",
      "min": 20,
      "max": 100,
      "step": 5,
      "unit": "%",
      "default": 100,
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.width_mobile == 'custom' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Padding"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding-block-start",
      "label": "Top",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 100,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 0
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding-block-end",
      "label": "Bottom",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 100,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 0
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding-inline-start",
      "label": "Left",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 100,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 0
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding-inline-end",
      "label": "Right",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 100,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 0
    }
  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "Mionas: email signup",
      "category": "t:categories.forms"
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
